<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
	<subsection xml:id="sec_tan_and_inverse_tan" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
		<title>The Tangent and the Inverse Tangent</title>
                <introduction>
                <p>
                                The tangent function is a little bit different from sine and cosine, because not only do values of the tangent repeat, but there are points where the tangent is not defined at all.
                </p>
                </introduction>
                <subsubsection xml:id='ssec_graph_of_tan'>
                        <title>The Graph of the Tangent Function</title>
                <p>
                        As shown in <xref ref='fig_tan'/>, the graph <m>y=\tan(x)</m> is undefined with vertical asymptotes at <m>x=-\frac{3}{2}\pi,-\frac{1}{2}\pi,\frac{1}{2}\pi,\frac{3}{2}\pi</m> and so forth. In fact for any whole number (or <term>integer</term>) <m>k</m>, <m>\tan(x)</m> is undefined at <m>x=k \pi +\frac{1}{2}\pi=\frac{(2k+1)}{2}\pi</m>. 
                        </p>
                        <p>
                                        As we come up on one of these <term>discontinuities</term> from the left side, values of the tangent grow larger and larger. We formally say that as <m>x\to\frac{1}{2}\pi</m>, for example, from the left, the tangent <em>increases without bound</em>, and sometimes we write <m>\tan(x)\to\infty</m> as <m>x\to\frac{1}{2}\pi^{-}</m>.
                </p>
                        <p>
                        Similarly, as we come up on one of these discontinuities from the right side, values of the tangent grow smaller and smaller, or more and more negative, and we say that as <m>x\to\frac{1}{2}\pi</m> from the right, the tangent <em>decreases without bound</em>, and sometimes we write <m>\tan(x)\to-\infty</m> as <m>x\to\frac{1}{2}\pi^{+}</m>.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id='fig_tan'>
                        <caption>The Tangent Function</caption>
                        <image width="50%">
                                <description><p>The graph of tangent with its principle branch, from minus pi over two to pi over two, emphasized</p></description>
                                <xi:include href="./principle_tan.xml" />
                        </image>
                </figure>
                </subsubsection>
                <subsubsection xml:id='ssec_principle_branch_tangent'>
                        <title>The Principle Branch of Tangent</title>
                        <exercise xml:id='exer_find_branch_tangent'>
                                <statement>
                                        <p>
                                                        Use the graph of <m>y=\tan(x)</m> in <xref ref='fig_tan'/> to find a subsection of the graph, <m>a\lt{}x\lt{}b</m>, that every real number value of <m>y</m> occurs exactly once in that range of <m>x</m> values.
                                        </p>
                                </statement>
                                <solution>
                                        <p>
                                                        One choice is <m>-\frac{1}{2}\pi\lt{}x\lt{}\frac{1}{2}\pi</m>. This is called the <term>principle branch</term> of tangent, sometimes written with a capital T, <m>\Tan(x)</m>.
                                        </p>
                                </solution>
                        </exercise>
                </subsubsection>
                <subsubsection xml:id='ssec_un_tan'>
                        <title>The <q>Un-Tangent</q></title>
                <p>
                                Now, we can find a function that undoes this branch of tangent: the <q>un-tangent</q> of a number <m>c</m> is the <em>unique</em> angle <m>\theta</m> so that: <m>\tan(\theta)=c</m> <alert>and</alert> <m>0\lt{}\theta\lt{}\pi</m>. For now, we will write <m>\untan(c)</m> and give the following definition.
                </p>
                <definition xml:id='def_untan'>
                        <title>The <q>Un-Tangent</q> Function</title>
                        <statement>
                                <p>
                                        For any real <m>x</m>, saying <m>\untan(x)=\theta</m> means that <m>\theta</m> is the (unique) angle so that
                                        <ol marker="(a)">
                                                <li><p><m>\tan(\theta)=x</m>, and</p></li>
                                                <li><p><m>-\frac{1}{2}\pi\lt{}\theta\lt{}\frac{1}{2}\pi</m>.</p></li>
                                        </ol>
                                </p>
                        </statement>
                </definition>
                <example xml:id='example_untan'>
                        <statement>
                                <p>
                                                We will find <m>\untan(x)</m> for several values of <m>x</m> using the unit circle.
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                        <ol>
                                                <li><p>To find <m>\untan\left(\sqrt{3}\right)</m>, we first locate points where the slope is <m>\sqrt{3}</m>, because <m>\tan(\theta)=\frac{y}{x}</m>. The points where the slope is <m>\sqrt{3}</m> are <m>\left(\frac{1}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m> at <m>\theta=\frac{\pi}{3}</m> and <m>\left(-\frac{1}{2},-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m> at <m>\theta=\frac{4\pi}{3}</m>. Since <m>0\lt{}\frac{\pi}{3}\lt{}\pi</m>, <md>\untan\left(\sqrt{3}\right)=\frac{\pi}{3}</md>.</p></li>
                                                <li><p>To find <m>\untan\left(-1\right)</m>, we first locate points where the slope is <m>-1</m>. The points where the slope is <m>-1</m> are <m>\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)</m> at <m>\theta=\frac{7\pi}{4}</m> and <m>\left(-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)</m> at <m>\theta=\frac{3\pi}{4}</m>. Neither of these lie in the interval<m>\left(-\frac{1}{2}\pi,\frac{1}{2}\pi\right)</m>, though. Instead, we will look at negative angles. The point at <m>\theta=\frac{7\pi}{4}</m> is also at <m>\theta=-\frac{\pi}{4}</m>. This is in the correct range, so <md>\untan\left(-1\right)=-\frac{\pi}{4}</md>.</p></li>
                                        </ol>
                                </p>
                        </statement>
                </example>
                </subsubsection>
                <subsubsection xml:id='ssec_arc_and_inv_tan'>
                        <title>Notation</title>
                        <p>
                                        The name we chose for our function, <q>un-tangent</q>, is intuitive, but it's not what the standard notational conventions are. Much more common are the <term>arc-tangent</term>, <m>\arctan(x)</m>, named this because we finding the angle in the unit circle is the same as finding the arc, and <term>inverse tangent</term>, which is confusingly written as <m>\tan^{-1}(x)</m>.
                        </p>
                </subsubsection>
                <subsubsection>
                        <title>Using the Inverse Tangent</title>
                        <example>
                                <statement>
                                        <p>
                                                We will write the vector <m>\vec{v}=\begin{bmatrix}-4\\-1\end{bmatrix}</m> in terms of its magnitude and its angle.
                                        </p>
                                        <p>
                                                <xref ref="fig_inv_tan" text="type-local"/> shows the vector <m>\vec{v}</m>, the unit circle, and the unit vector <m>\vec{u}</m>, the unit vector that points in the direction of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
                                                </p>
                                                <p>
                                                        To find this unit vector, we calculate <m>\vec{u}=\frac{1}{\Abs{\vec{v}}}\vec{v}</m>. So,
                                                        <md>
                                                                <mrow>
                                                                        \Abs{\vec{v}}
                                                                                &amp;=
                                                                        \sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}
                                                                </mrow>
                                                                <mrow>
                                                                                &amp;=
                                                                        \sqrt{16+1}
                                                                </mrow>
                                                                <mrow>
                                                                                &amp;=
                                                                        \sqrt{17},
                                                                </mrow>
                                                        </md>
                                                        and so
                                                        <md>
                                                                <mrow>
                                                                        \vec{u}
                                                                                &amp;=\frac{1}{\sqrt{17}}\begin{bmatrix}-4\\-1\end{bmatrix}
                                                                </mrow>
                                                                <mrow>
                                                                                &amp;=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{-4}{\sqrt{17}}\\\frac{-1}{\sqrt{17}}\end{bmatrix}
                                                                </mrow>
                                                        </md>
                                        </p>
                                        <figure xml:id="fig_inv_tan">
                                                <caption>The vector <m>\vec{v}</m> represented as a point and the unit circle.</caption>
                                                <image width="50%">
                                                        <description>
                                                                <p>
                                                                        A set of axes with x from -4 to 4 and y from -2 to 4, with the unit circle, the point (-4, -1), with a line drawn through (-4,-1) and (0,0) and extended to (4,1). The angle theta sub star from the x axis counterclockwise to the segment from (0,0) to (4,1) and the angle theta from the x axis counterclockwise to the segment from (0,0) to (-4, -1) are shown. 
                                                                </p>
                                                        </description>
                                                        <xi:include href="./invtan.xml"/>
                                                </image>
                                        </figure>
                                        <p>
                                                        Now we need to find the angle, <m>\theta</m>, so that <m>\cos(\theta)=\frac{-4}{\sqrt{17}}</m> and <m>\sin(\theta)=\frac{-1}{\sqrt{17}}</m>. We will do this by noting that <m>\tan(\theta)=\left(\frac{-1}{\sqrt{17}}\right)/\left(\frac{-4}{\sqrt{17}}\right)=(-1)/(-4)=1/4</m>. It is worth noting for future reference that the tangent can be obtained directly from the original vector <m>\vec{v}</m> by dividing the second component by the first.
                                        </p>
                                        <p>
                                                        We calculate <m>\theta_{*}=\arctan(1/4)\approx{}0.24498</m>. This angle is in the first quadrant and is our <term>reference angle</term>. Because of the periodicity of tangent, the angle for our vector is <m>\theta=\pi+\theta_{*}\approx{}3.38657</m>.
                                        </p>
                                        <p>
                                                We check our answer by calculating 
                                                <md>
                                                        <mrow>
                                                                \sqrt{17}\begin{bmatrix}\cos(3.38657)\\ \sin(3.38657)\end{bmatrix}
                                                                &amp;=\begin{bmatrix}\sqrt{17}\cos(3.38657)\\ \sqrt{17}\sin(3.38657)\end{bmatrix}
                                                        </mrow>
                                                        <mrow>
                                                                &amp;=\begin{bmatrix}-4\\ -1\end{bmatrix}
                                                        </mrow>
                                                </md>
                                        </p>
                                </statement>
                        </example>
                </subsubsection>
	</subsection>

